  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation $\u03d5=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?",
  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\u03d5\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\u03d5=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/ee967c13fa7edaaba4d3a218abf20aa067cbd2251f2a008158c9c991c27344ea.png)",
